Git is a tool that allows you to Version Control your file system. 

What is a Version Control System?
A Version Control System (VCS) is a software tool that tracks and manages modifications to source code and files over time. It acts like a smart project time machine, saving precise
snapshots of your work so that you can review modifications, revert to earlier versions, and collaborate without overwriting anyone else's code.

Core Benefits of Version Control
Complete Revision History: Records exactly who made a change, what lines were altered, when it happened, and why.
Safe Experimentation: Features like branching let you duplicate the main code to test new features or fix bugs independently.
Conflict Resolution: Allows multiple developers to work on the same file simultaneously and provides structured tools to safely merge their updates.
Disaster Recovery: Serves as a secure backup system so you can instantly roll back an entire project if a catastrophic error occurs.

Centralized vs. Distributed Systems
Version control systems generally fall into two architectural categories:

Feature                  Centralized VCS (CVCS)                                                      Distributed VCS (DVCS)
How it Works             Files are stored on a single central server.                               Every user clones a full copy of the entire project history onto their local machine. 
                         Users check out individual files to work on them.
Offline Capability       Highly limited; requires an active internet connection                     Fully operational offline; you can commit changes and view full history anywhere.
                         to save history or commit updates.
Resilience               Has a single point of failure; if the server crashes,                      Highly resilient; every local copy acts as a complete backup of the repository.
                         collaboration stops and data risks loss.
Popular Examples         Apache Subversion (SVN) and Perforce Helix Core.                           Git and Mercurial.

How to install Git?
To install Git, you need to download the installer or run a specific terminal command depending on your operating system.

macOS Installation
1. Using Homebrew (Recommended): Open your Terminal and run:
   brew install git
2. Using Xcode Tools: Open your Terminal and type:
   git --version

Linux Installation
Open your terminal and run the command that matches your Linux distribution:
sudo apt update
sudo apt install git

Next Step: First-Time SetupOnce installed, you must introduce yourself to Git so your future code changes are properly credited to you. Run these two commands in your terminal:
git config --global user.name "Your Name"
git config --global user.email "your.email@example.com"

Initializing Git
To initialize a brand-new Git repository on your computer, you need to open your terminal (or Git Bash on Windows) and run a single core command inside your project folder.

Step-by-Step Initialization
1. Navigate to your project folder: Use the cd command to move into the directory where your project files are located (or where you want to create them).
cd path/to/your/project-folder

2. Initialize the repository: Run the initialization command. This creates a hidden .git folder that tracks your project history.
git init

3. Set the default branch name (Highly Recommended): Modern development uses main as the default branch name instead of the older default master. Set it by running:
git branch -M main

Saving Your First Files
After initialization, Git is watching your folder, but it is not tracking any files yet. To save your first snapshot, run these three commands in order:

Check the project status: See which files Git notices but isn't tracking yet.
git status

Stage your files: Tell Git to prepare all files (.) in the folder for a snapshot.
git add .

Commit your changes: Save the snapshot permanently to your local history with a descriptive message.
git commit -m "Initial commit"


